{
	title: 'A <question> tag marks a turn that waits for the user',
	spec: 'A reply asking the user ends with <question>one line</question> instead of <summary>. Both clients show question content inline at its original position, without XML syntax, including transcript replay; summary content remains notification metadata unless it is the only answer. Incomplete notification tags stay hidden during streaming; literal tags in Markdown code remain literal. The question supplies an attention notification and a persistent tab attention mark. Invariant: a question is visible and stays open until answered (task 6q5 defines answering); messages arriving meanwhile do not hold behind it. Slash commands keep their existing command behavior. Waiting derives from history, with no new state store, tool, or question UI. SYSTEM.md says a reply needing a user answer ends with <question>the question</question> instead; human interaction is needed to continue. Both clients share the behavior. Raised in sessions 166-gig and 165-gab on 2026-10-06 and refined in 167-fox on 2026-10-07; a separate tag avoids unreliable prose classification and summary attributes.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"summary.ts handles both tags with one regex; summary.asks(text) is true when the last complete tag is <question>. notify.asked(id) reads the latest turn's last text block from pages (history), and gates three places: notify.kind (attention instead of done), prompts.next and prompts.submit (a queued submit to an idle session after an asking turn stays queued), and tabs (tab-seen does not clear the mark; a busy state event does).",
		'The initial design hid questions like redundant summaries and only held queue:true messages. Ordinary agent reports could start a fresh turn and erase the history-derived wait. Preserve question text in shared answer rendering and classify incoming agent messages as queued while asking; keep the existing inbox and human overrides.',
